# Kotlin/Android security

Narrows `core-common-rules`' stack-agnostic security rules to Android-
specific, OWASP Mobile-relevant risks. Applies only to `*.kt`/`*.kts`
files.

## Secrets and credential storage

- Never store a credential, auth token, or API secret in plain
  `SharedPreferences` — plain `SharedPreferences` is an unencrypted XML
  file readable on a rooted device or via backup extraction. Prefer the
  Android Keystore directly (generate/store the key in the Keystore, use
  it to encrypt the value yourself) over `EncryptedSharedPreferences`
  (Jetpack `androidx.security:security-crypto`) for new code — that
  library has known, unresolved reliability issues (a corrupted-keyset
  failure mode with no clean recovery path) and Google's own guidance has
  moved toward not recommending it for new applications; verify the
  library's current status in the Android docs before reaching for it in
  an existing codebase that already depends on it.
- Never hard-code an API key, client secret, or signing credential as a
  Kotlin string literal or a `BuildConfig` field, whether the value is
  committed or injected at build time from a local, untracked properties
  file (`local.properties`) or a CI secret — either way, a `BuildConfig`
  field is compiled into the APK exactly like a string literal and is
  just as recoverable by decompiling it. Keeping the value out of source
  control (via `local.properties`/a CI secret) only stops it leaking
  through the repository; it does not keep it off the device. For a
  value that must actually stay off the device, fetch it from a backend
  at runtime instead of baking it into the build at all.
- Generate and store cryptographic keys through `AndroidKeyStore`
  (`KeyGenParameterSpec`) rather than deriving or hard-coding a key in
  application code — the Keystore keeps key material out of the app's
  own process memory and out of extractable storage.

## Manifest and component exposure

- Every `<activity>`, `<service>`, `<receiver>`, and `<provider>` in
  `AndroidManifest.xml` that does not need to be reachable from other
  apps should have `android:exported="false"` (or no `exported`
  attribute with no intent-filter, matching the modern default); a
  component left `exported="true"` with no permission check is callable
  by any app on the device, including one crafted to pass malformed
  input.
- An exported component that must stay exported (a deep-link entry point,
  a share target) validates every piece of the incoming `Intent` before
  acting on it — never trusts an extra, a URI path segment, or a
  `ContentProvider` query purely because the intent-filter matched.
- Guard a `ContentProvider` and any exported component with a
  signature-level custom permission when the caller is meant to be your
  own other app, not "any app that asks."

## Secure intent and deep-link handling

- Treat every value pulled from an incoming `Intent` (extras, deep-link
  URI segments/query params, a `ClipData` from a share) as untrusted
  input — validate its shape and range before using it to navigate, query
  a database, or construct a file path; do not assume it matches what
  your own app would have sent.
- Prefer an explicit `Intent` (naming the target component) for
  inter-component communication within your own app; use an implicit
  `Intent` only when you genuinely need another app to handle it, and
  set the package (`setPackage`) when you only want your own app to
  respond, to avoid intent interception by another installed app.
- Validate a deep-link's scheme/host/path against an allowlist before
  using any part of it to load content or trigger an action —
  `App Links`/verified `https` deep links reduce but do not eliminate
  this: a query parameter or path segment can still carry attacker-
  controlled data even through a verified link.

## Data and transport

- Use `HttpsURLConnection`/an HTTP client configured to require TLS for
  every network call carrying credentials or personal data; do not add a
  custom `TrustManager`/`HostnameVerifier` that accepts all certificates
  outside of a short-lived local debug build, and never ship one that
  way.
- Mark a view/field that must not appear in screenshots or the recent-
  apps app-switcher preview (a PIN entry, payment details) with
  `FLAG_SECURE` on the window, rather than relying on the OS default.
- Avoid logging PII, tokens, or full request/response bodies with
  `Log.d`/`Log.i` in release builds — strip or gate verbose logging
  behind a debug-only build flag so production logs (which can be pulled
  from a device or bug report) do not leak sensitive data.
